VALUES
  ('cs_griffin_geography', 'plc_griffin', 'geography', 'Geography',
   'Nomenclature [ edit ] Etymology [ edit ] Griffin depicted on obverse side of coin. —Silver tetradrachm . Abdera ( c. 450–430BC). &#91; 1 &#93; &#91; a &#93; The derivation of this word remains uncertain. It could be related to the Greek word γρυπός (grypos), meaning ''curved'', or ''hooked''. Greek γρύφ (gryph) from γρύφ ''hook-nosed'' is suggested. &#91; 2 &#93; It could also have been an Anatolian loan word derived from a Semitic language; compare the Hebrew כרוב kərúv . &#91; 3 &#93; &#91; 4 &#93; Persian names [ edit ] Shirdal on the silver cup, Iranian Art . In the modern Persian language , the griffin has come to be called šērdāl ( Persian : شیردال ), meaning ''lion-eagle''. However, the practice of referring to ancient Iranian griffin objects or monuments as sherdal , &#91; 5 &#93; is not followed by other current archaeological scholarship (e.g., here &#91; 6 &#93; ). Possible Old or Middle Iranian names for the creature have been discussed. Middle Persian Sēnmurw in Sasanian culture was a fabulous composite creature, and Russian archaeologist Boris A. Litvinskij &#160;&#91; ru &#93; argued for the possibility that the application of this term may extend to the griffin. &#91; 7 &#93; &#91; 8 &#93; The term Sēnmurw is recognized as the etymological ancestor of simurgh , which is generally regarded as a mythological bird (rather than a composite) in later medieval Persian literature, &#91; 9 &#93; &#91; b &#93; though some argue that this bird may have originated from the Mesopotamian lion-griffin. &#91; 10 &#93; There is also the Armenian term Paskuč ( Armenian : պասկուչ ) that had been used to translate Greek gryp ''griffin'' in the Septuagint , &#91; 11 &#93; which H. P. Schmidt characterized as the counterpart of the simurgh. &#91; 9 &#93; However, the cognate term Baškuč (glossed as ''griffin'') also occurs in Middle Persian, attested in the Zoroastrian cosmological text Bundahishn XXIV (supposedly distinguishable from Sēnmurw which also appears in the same text). &#', '["clm_wiki_griffin_geography"]', '["src_en_wikipedia"]',
   'general', 'en', 'assisted', 'places_ingest_v1', 'auto_approved', 1, unixepoch(), unixepoch(),
   30, 1, 1, unixepoch());

